Here's the full migration analysis and replacement code.

## Pattern Classification

| Location | Pattern | Issue in .NET 8 |
|----------|---------|-----------------|
| `_thread.Interrupt()` | Blocking call interruption | `SYSLIB0046` (obsolete in .NET 9, unreliable in .NET 8) |
| `_thread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop fallback | `PlatformNotSupportedException` — **will crash** |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | Blocking call | Not cancellation-aware |
| `while (true)` | Work loop | No cooperative exit path |

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly ManualResetEvent _newEvent = new ManualResetEvent(false);
    private readonly Queue<string> _queue = new Queue<string>();
    private Thread? _thread;
    private readonly object _lock = new object();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        var handles = new WaitHandle[] { _newEvent, _cts.Token.WaitHandle };

        while (!_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Wakes on either: new item enqueued, OR cancellation requested
            int signaled = WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles);

            if (signaled == 1) // cancellation token fired
                break;

            _newEvent.Reset();

            string? item = null;
            lock (_lock)
            {
                if (_queue.Count > 0)
                    item = _queue.Dequeue();
            }

            if (item is not null)
                Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        lock (_lock) { _queue.Enqueue(item); }
        _newEvent.Set();
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();           // wakes WaitAny via token.WaitHandle
        _thread?.Join(5000);     // wait for graceful exit
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _newEvent.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Key Changes

| Before | After | Why |
|--------|-------|-----|
| `while (true)` | `while (!_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)` | Cooperative exit |
| `_newEvent.WaitOne()` | `WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles)` | Wakes on cancellation **or** new work |
| `_thread.Interrupt()` | `_cts.Cancel()` | Token's WaitHandle unblocks WaitAny |
| `_thread.Abort()` | *(removed)* | Cannot force-kill threads in .NET 8 |
| *(none)* | `IDisposable` | CTS and WaitHandle must be disposed |

## Pitfalls to Watch

1. **`Thread.Abort` will crash** — it throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`, not `ThreadAbortException`. This is the most critical issue.

2. **Race in the original code** — if multiple items are enqueued before `WaitOne` returns, `Reset()` clears the signal and subsequent items are lost. The migrated version adds a `_queue.Count > 0` guard. Consider draining all items per wake-up for correctness.

3. **Cancellation isn't instant** — if `Handle(item)` is long-running, `Stop()` blocks until it finishes. Pass `_cts.Token` into `Handle()` if it needs to be interruptible.

4. **Dispose the `CancellationTokenSource`** — leaking it causes timer/callback leaks. The `IDisposable` pattern ensures cleanup.

5. **`Thread.Interrupt` is also obsolete** — marked `SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9 and unreliable even in .NET 8. Don't use it as a halfway migration step.
